            'intro' => 'Most organisations do not need more AI noise. They need a practical view of where AI can improve a Workday process, what data and governance are required, who should own the decision and how users will trust the outcome. Workday AI should be assessed through business value, risk and readiness.',
            'facts' => [
                [ 'label' => 'Definition', 'value' => 'Use of Workday AI, automation and analytics to support decisions and reduce manual work.' ],
                [ 'label' => 'Primary purpose', 'value' => 'Improve process efficiency, planning quality, insight and user experience.' ],
                [ 'label' => 'Core controls', 'value' => 'Data quality, permissions, transparency, human oversight and change management.' ],
                [ 'label' => 'Best starting point', 'value' => 'A clear business problem with trusted data and measurable benefit.' ],
                [ 'label' => 'Success measure', 'value' => 'Responsible adoption that improves work without reducing trust.' ],
            ],
            'sections' => [
                [ 'section_heading' => 'What Workday AI should mean', 'section_body' => 'Workday AI should not mean adding disconnected tools around the platform. It should mean using Workday data, automation, analytics and AI-supported features to improve real workflows. Good use cases reduce manual checks, surface patterns, improve planning, accelerate decisions or make self-service easier.' ],
                [ 'section_heading' => 'From AI hype to business value', 'section_body' => 'The right starting point is the business problem. In HCM, that might be skills visibility, hiring workflow efficiency, manager guidance or workforce planning. In Finance and Planning, it might be forecasting, anomaly detection, scenario planning or faster close activity. AI is valuable only when it improves a decision or removes avoidable work.' ],
                [ 'section_heading' => 'Where AI can support HCM', 'section_body' => 'In HCM, AI can support skills intelligence, talent processes, recruiting workflows, manager self-service, workforce planning and employee experience. Each use case needs clear ownership, trusted data and a decision about where human review remains necessary.' ],
                [ 'section_heading' => 'Where AI can support Finance and Planning', 'section_body' => 'In Finance and Adaptive Planning, AI and analytics can help identify anomalies, sharpen forecasts, compare scenarios, support planning cycles and reduce manual review. These benefits depend on clean structures, consistent definitions and confidence in source data.' ],
                [ 'section_heading' => 'Responsible adoption and governance', 'section_body' => 'Responsible adoption should cover privacy, security, transparency, explainability, bias risk, human oversight and change impact. Teams should know what data is used, who can access the output, how decisions are reviewed and where the AI-supported process fits into business governance.' ],
                [ 'section_heading' => 'Readiness before activation', 'section_body' => 'Before activating AI capability, teams should check data quality, reporting trust, process stability, permission design, integration dependencies, user readiness and support ownership. AI will not fix weak foundations. It performs best where the Workday tenant is clean, governed and understood.' ],
                [ 'section_heading' => 'How to choose the first use cases', 'section_body' => 'Prioritise use cases with a clear pain point, trusted data, measurable benefit, manageable risk and an owner who can drive adoption. Avoid activating features simply because they are available. A smaller governed use case with measurable benefit is better than a broad rollout that users do not trust.' ],
                [ 'section_heading' => 'How Zeneesha helps with Workday AI', 'section_body' => 'Zeneesha helps organisations review existing Workday capability, identify practical AI and automation opportunities, assess readiness and activate improvements responsibly. The focus is measurable value, clear governance and user trust rather than technology theatre.' ],
            ],
            'faq' => [
                [ 'faq_q' => 'How is AI used in Workday?', 'faq_a' => 'AI in Workday can support areas such as skills intelligence, talent workflows, manager self-service, forecasting, anomaly detection, planning and decision support depending on the modules and configuration in use.' ],
                [ 'faq_q' => 'Should every Workday AI feature be activated?', 'faq_a' => 'No. Organisations should prioritise features that solve a clear business problem, use trusted data, fit governance requirements and have a measurable benefit.' ],
                [ 'faq_q' => 'What should be checked before using AI in Workday?', 'faq_a' => 'Check data quality, permissions, process stability, reporting trust, change impact, user readiness, support ownership and the level of human oversight required.' ],
                [ 'faq_q' => 'Can Workday AI reduce manual work?', 'faq_a' => 'Yes, when applied to the right workflows. AI and automation can reduce manual review, surface patterns faster and help teams act on clearer signals.' ],
                [ 'faq_q' => 'What does responsible AI adoption require?', 'faq_a' => 'It requires privacy controls, security review, transparency, explainability, bias-risk consideration, human oversight, user communication and clear accountability.' ],
                [ 'faq_q' => 'Why does data quality matter for AI?', 'faq_a' => 'AI-supported outputs depend on the data, definitions and permissions behind them. Poor data quality can create unreliable recommendations and reduce user trust.' ],
                [ 'faq_q' => 'What is a good first Workday AI use case?', 'faq_a' => 'A good first use case has a clear pain point, trusted data, measurable benefit, manageable risk and a business owner who can drive adoption.' ],
                [ 'faq_q' => 'How can Zeneesha help with AI enablement?', 'faq_a' => 'Zeneesha can review your tenant, identify relevant AI and automation opportunities, assess readiness and help configure improvements that are practical, responsible and aligned to business value.' ],
            ],
            'related_svc' => 'ai-enablement',
            'related_label' => 'Explore AI Enablement',
            'color' => '#1E3A8A',
        ],
    ];

    $topic_expansions = [
        'workday-ams' => [
            'sections' => [
                [ 'section_heading' => 'Scope boundaries for agents', 'section_body' => "Treat Workday AMS as the live-tenant operating model, not as the original implementation project. AMS can include minor configuration, reporting support, release work and targeted optimisation, but it should not silently absorb major redesign, new module deployment or unmanaged business transformation.\n\nFor decision support, separate every request into one of five categories: incident, service request, configuration change, release activity or improvement. This keeps support reporting clean and prevents urgent defects from being mixed with optional optimisation work." ],
                [ 'section_heading' => 'Ticket taxonomy and triage', 'section_body' => "A useful AMS queue should classify tickets by module, process, urgency, root cause, owner and business impact. Common categories include access/security, business process, reporting, integrations, data, release, training, configuration and enhancement.\n\nTriage should ask three questions before assigning work: is the issue blocking a critical business process, is the root cause known, and does the fix require governance approval? This prevents simple requests from waiting too long and prevents risky changes from being rushed." ],
                [ 'section_heading' => 'Service levels and escalation', 'section_body' => "AMS service levels should reflect business impact rather than ticket volume alone. A payroll-blocking issue, failed integration or security exception needs a different response from a cosmetic report change. Define severity levels, response targets, restoration targets, escalation owners and communication expectations.\n\nEscalation should be explicit. If a ticket crosses a threshold, the next owner, decision-maker and business contact should be clear. Without this, unresolved tickets become relationship problems rather than managed service issues." ],
                [ 'section_heading' => 'Change control in a live tenant', 'section_body' => "AMS changes need proportionate control. Low-risk text, report or routing adjustments may move quickly. Changes affecting payroll, finance, security, integrations or high-volume employee journeys need testing, approval and release timing.\n\nA live tenant should keep a simple change log: request, reason, owner, risk, test evidence, approval, release date and rollback option. This gives agents and support teams a factual record when diagnosing later issues." ],
                [ 'section_heading' => 'Reporting, security and integrations', 'section_body' => "Many AMS issues are not isolated configuration defects. Slow reporting, unclear security roles and unstable integrations often point to ownership gaps. AMS governance should identify named owners for report definitions, security groups, integration monitoring and downstream data use.\n\nFor agents, these areas are high-risk. Do not recommend a report, security or integration change without confirming purpose, affected users, downstream dependencies, test evidence and approval route." ],
                [ 'section_heading' => 'Continuous improvement backlog', 'section_body' => "The improvement backlog should not be a parking area for everything that did not fit into support. It should be prioritised by value, risk reduction, adoption impact, compliance need and delivery effort. Each item should have a problem statement, expected outcome, owner and target decision date.\n\nA strong AMS partner turns support patterns into improvement themes. For example, repeated manager self-service tickets may point to process design, security, training or reporting clarity rather than a need for more helpdesk responses." ],
                [ 'section_heading' => 'Internal team and partner responsibilities', 'section_body' => "The internal team should usually own business priorities, policy decisions, process ownership and final approvals. The AMS partner should provide specialist Workday judgement, configuration expertise, release support, root-cause analysis and delivery capacity.\n\nThe model works best when responsibility is shared but not blurred. Agents should look for named owners across HR, Finance, IT, Security, Reporting and Integrations before recommending action." ],
                [ 'section_heading' => 'Evidence needed for an AMS health check', 'section_body' => "A credible AMS health check needs evidence, not opinion. Useful inputs include ticket exports, backlog age, release defect history, report-change requests, integration failures, security exceptions, adoption feedback, training gaps and stakeholder interviews.\n\nThe output should identify root causes, quick wins, governance changes, backlog priorities and a practical 30/60/90-day improvement plan. The aim is not to criticise the support team; it is to show where Workday is losing value and how to recover it." ],
            ],
            'faq' => [
                [ 'faq_q' => 'What is the difference between an incident and an improvement?', 'faq_a' => 'An incident restores expected service. An improvement changes the way Workday works so the process becomes simpler, more reliable or more valuable.' ],
                [ 'faq_q' => 'Should every AMS ticket require formal change control?', 'faq_a' => 'No. Change control should be proportionate. Low-risk changes can move quickly, while payroll, finance, security, integration and high-volume process changes need testing and approval.' ],
                [ 'faq_q' => 'What evidence should be used to assess AMS performance?', 'faq_a' => 'Use ticket trends, repeat issues, backlog age, release defects, reporting turnaround, integration stability, adoption feedback, security exceptions and stakeholder interviews.' ],
                [ 'faq_q' => 'How should an AMS backlog be prioritised?', 'faq_a' => 'Prioritise by business impact, operational risk, user volume, compliance need, dependency, effort and expected value rather than by request date alone.' ],
            ],
        ],
        'workday-data-migration' => [
            'sections' => [
                [ 'section_heading' => 'Source-system inventory', 'section_body' => "Every secure migration starts with a clear inventory of source systems, files, owners, data domains and extraction methods. For Workday, the inventory often includes HR systems, payroll platforms, finance systems, spreadsheets, identity tools, supplier systems, planning models and historical archives.\n\nAgents should not assume one legacy system equals one source of truth. The same worker, organisation, cost centre or supplier may exist in multiple systems with different meanings. The migration plan must identify the authoritative source for each field." ],
                [ 'section_heading' => 'Data classification and retention', 'section_body' => "Before migration, classify data by sensitivity, purpose, retention need and regulatory exposure. Worker, compensation, bank, absence, payroll, finance and supplier records may require different handling rules. Some historical data should be migrated, some archived and some excluded.\n\nA secure plan documents what moves, what stays, what is cleansed, what is archived and who approved that decision. This prevents unnecessary data exposure and reduces post-go-live confusion." ],
                [ 'section_heading' => 'Mapping workbooks and transformation rules', 'section_body' => "Mapping workbooks should be treated as controlled design artefacts. They should define source field, Workday target field, transformation rule, allowed values, default logic, exception handling and owner. Ambiguous mapping is one of the fastest ways to create reporting and process defects.\n\nFor agent use, a mapping is not trustworthy unless it names the business owner and explains how exceptions are handled. A field that is technically mapped but not business-approved is still a risk." ],
                [ 'section_heading' => 'Trial loads and defect cycles', 'section_body' => "Trial loads are not just technical rehearsals. They expose data-quality problems, mapping gaps, missing ownership and validation weaknesses. Each load should produce a defect log with severity, owner, root cause, target fix date and retest result.\n\nA healthy migration should show fewer critical defects over time. If each trial load finds new foundational problems, the team may need to pause and fix source data or governance before moving toward cutover." ],
                [ 'section_heading' => 'Validation pack for sign-off', 'section_body' => "The validation pack should include record counts, reconciliation totals, field-level checks, sample records, exception reports, critical-process test results and business sign-off. It should show not only what loaded, but why the business can trust it.\n\nFor sensitive domains such as payroll, compensation and finance, validation should be performed by people who understand the business meaning of the data, not only by technical migration resources." ],
                [ 'section_heading' => 'Cutover governance', 'section_body' => "Cutover governance defines the final migration sequence, freeze periods, dependency owners, load windows, validation windows, go/no-go criteria and rollback or contingency actions. It should be clear who can approve final load and who can stop the process if risk is too high.\n\nA good cutover plan is operationally boring. Everyone knows the order, the decision points, the communication route and the evidence required before moving forward." ],
                [ 'section_heading' => 'Common failure modes', 'section_body' => "Common failure modes include undocumented spreadsheets, duplicate worker records, old organisation structures, inconsistent effective dates, missing owners, over-migration of historical data, untested transformation rules and late changes to mapping.\n\nThese issues are rarely solved by another technical load alone. They need ownership, cleansing decisions and business sign-off." ],
                [ 'section_heading' => 'Agent checklist for secure migration', 'section_body' => "Before recommending migration action, confirm source owner, data owner, target field, transformation rule, sensitivity level, validation method, exception owner and sign-off requirement. If any of these are missing, the answer should flag the gap rather than claim the migration is ready.\n\nThe safe recommendation is always evidence-led: profile first, cleanse next, map carefully, test repeatedly, validate with the business and reconcile after load." ],
            ],
            'faq' => [
                [ 'faq_q' => 'What is a migration validation pack?', 'faq_a' => 'It is the evidence set used for sign-off, usually including record counts, reconciliation totals, sample checks, exception reports, test results and business approval.' ],
                [ 'faq_q' => 'Should all historical data be migrated?', 'faq_a' => 'No. Historical data should be migrated only when there is a business, reporting, legal or operational reason. Otherwise it may be better archived or excluded.' ],
                [ 'faq_q' => 'What is the biggest migration risk?', 'faq_a' => 'The biggest risk is loading data that is technically valid but not trusted by the business because ownership, meaning or validation was unclear.' ],
                [ 'faq_q' => 'What should agents check before calling migration ready?', 'faq_a' => 'Check data ownership, field mapping, transformation rules, sensitivity controls, trial-load results, defect status, validation evidence and business sign-off.' ],
            ],
        ],
        'workday-mid-market' => [
            'sections' => [
                [ 'section_heading' => 'Support model options', 'section_body' => "Mid-market organisations usually choose between internal ownership, retained specialist support, module-specific support or a broader AMS model. The right choice depends on tenant complexity, internal Workday experience, number of modules, release workload and tolerance for operational risk.\n\nA small internal team can own the operating model, but it should not be expected to cover every configuration, reporting, integration, release and security question without senior backup." ],
                [ 'section_heading' => 'In-house versus partner support', 'section_body' => "In-house teams understand the business context, policy and stakeholder relationships. A partner adds specialist depth, external pattern recognition and surge capacity. The best model combines both: internal ownership for priorities and decisions, partner support for Workday expertise and delivery.\n\nAgents should avoid recommending full outsourcing by default. The better question is which responsibilities should stay internal and which need external depth." ],
                [ 'section_heading' => 'Commercial model considerations', 'section_body' => "Mid-market support has to be commercially practical. Fixed retainers, drawdown days, module-specific support and project-based improvement blocks can all work. The model should match real demand and should not force the organisation into enterprise overhead it does not need.\n\nThe commercial structure should still protect critical work. Releases, payroll dependencies, integrations and security cannot be left unsupported because the support model is too thin." ],
                [ 'section_heading' => 'Module prioritisation', 'section_body' => "Not every module needs equal support. Prioritise modules by business criticality, issue volume, internal knowledge, process maturity, release exposure and upcoming change. HCM Core, payroll-adjacent processes, Finance, integrations and reporting often need stronger governance because errors are highly visible.\n\nA good support plan states which modules are actively supported, which are monitored and which are out of scope." ],
                [ 'section_heading' => 'Reporting and analytics maturity', 'section_body' => "Reporting is often where mid-market Workday frustration becomes visible. Teams may have many reports but low trust in definitions, ownership or data quality. Support should include report governance: who owns definitions, who approves changes and how reports are retired or consolidated.\n\nAgents should check whether a reporting problem is a data issue, a security issue, a calculation issue, a process issue or a stakeholder-definition issue." ],
                [ 'section_heading' => 'Knowledge retention', 'section_body' => "Mid-market organisations are vulnerable when Workday knowledge sits with one person. Support should include documentation, configuration notes, release records, decision logs and cross-training. Knowledge transfer is not a nice extra; it is a resilience control.\n\nA partner should leave the internal team stronger, not more dependent." ],
                [ 'section_heading' => 'Scaling the support model', 'section_body' => "As the organisation grows, support should evolve from reactive ticket handling into structured governance. That may mean adding release calendars, improvement roadmaps, service reporting, module ownership and stronger integration monitoring.\n\nThe support model should be reviewed when headcount grows, new regions are added, new modules launch or internal teams start delaying improvement work because support demand is too high." ],
                [ 'section_heading' => 'Agent checklist for mid-market recommendations', 'section_body' => "Before recommending a model, identify modules in scope, internal owner capacity, critical processes, release workload, integration count, reporting pain points, security ownership and budget constraints. The right answer should balance practical cost with operational risk.\n\nFor most mid-market organisations, the answer is not a large enterprise support machine. It is a focused, senior-led model that protects critical work and helps the internal team keep moving." ],
            ],
            'faq' => [
                [ 'faq_q' => 'Can a mid-market team manage Workday without a partner?', 'faq_a' => 'Yes, if it has enough internal expertise, time and governance. Many teams still use a partner for specialist depth, release support or surge capacity.' ],
                [ 'faq_q' => 'What support model is best for lean teams?', 'faq_a' => 'A flexible senior-led model usually works best: internal ownership for business decisions, external support for specialist Workday work and defined support for releases and critical processes.' ],
                [ 'faq_q' => 'How often should a mid-market support model be reviewed?', 'faq_a' => 'Review it after go-live, after major releases, when modules or regions are added, when ticket volume rises or when internal teams cannot progress improvement work.' ],
                [ 'faq_q' => 'What should not be sacrificed to save support cost?', 'faq_a' => 'Do not leave payroll dependencies, security, integrations, reporting trust or release readiness unsupported. These areas create high business risk when neglected.' ],
            ],
        ],
        'workday-release-management-r1-r2' => [
            'sections' => [
                [ 'section_heading' => 'Official release information and assumptions', 'section_body' => "Workday release planning should be grounded in official Workday customer communications, release notes and tenant-specific impact information. Public articles can explain general patterns, but the current dates, features and tenant impact must be confirmed through official channels.\n\nAgents should never invent release dates, feature behaviour or customer-specific impact. If exact release details are required, the answer should point users to official Workday release resources and internal tenant review." ],
                [ 'section_heading' => 'Release calendar and milestones', 'section_body' => "A release calendar should work backwards from production update dates. It should include release-note review, impact assessment, test-script updates, regression testing, defect triage, stakeholder sign-off, communications, production readiness and post-release monitoring.\n\nThe calendar should also identify blackout dates, payroll or finance close windows, audit periods and other business constraints that affect testing or deployment timing." ],
                [ 'section_heading' => 'Impact assessment matrix', 'section_body' => "Impact assessment should classify changes by module, process, user group, integration, report, security role and business risk. Each item should be tagged as mandatory readiness, optional adoption, monitor only or not relevant.\n\nThis matrix gives leaders a clear view of where testing effort should go. It also prevents teams from spending time on low-impact features while missing high-risk operational changes." ],
                [ 'section_heading' => 'Regression test library', 'section_body' => "A regression library should cover critical Workday journeys: hire, change job, terminate, absence, compensation, payroll-adjacent steps, supplier or finance processes, approvals, reporting, security and integrations. The library should be reviewed every release because processes and business priorities change.\n\nAgents should not recommend a generic test plan without identifying critical tenant journeys and risk areas." ],
                [ 'section_heading' => 'Defect management during release', 'section_body' => "Release defects should be logged with severity, affected process, owner, target fix, workaround, retest status and go/no-go impact. Not every defect blocks release readiness, but every material defect needs a clear decision.\n\nThe release team should distinguish Workday-delivered changes, customer configuration issues, test-data issues and training gaps. Each requires a different response." ],
                [ 'section_heading' => 'Communications by audience', 'section_body' => "Release communications should be role-specific. HR operations may need process notes, managers may need self-service changes, Finance may need close-cycle assurance, integration teams may need technical dependencies and executives may need risk and readiness status.\n\nA single generic announcement is rarely enough for meaningful adoption or readiness." ],
                [ 'section_heading' => 'Feature adoption backlog', 'section_body' => "Not every new capability should be adopted immediately. Create a feature adoption backlog that records potential value, effort, risk, owner, prerequisites and target release window. This turns release review into a continuous improvement input rather than a one-time compliance task.\n\nGood release management protects operations first and adopts selectively second." ],
                [ 'section_heading' => 'Agent checklist for release advice', 'section_body' => "Before giving release guidance, confirm official release source, tenant modules, critical processes, integration dependencies, testing owners, sign-off route, business blackout dates and communication needs. If those facts are unknown, the answer should flag assumptions instead of giving false certainty.\n\nThe safest release advice is to focus testing where failure would hurt the business most." ],
            ],
            'faq' => [
                [ 'faq_q' => 'Can an agent state exact Workday release dates?', 'faq_a' => 'Only if the date is verified from official Workday release resources or the customer tenant communications. Otherwise it should avoid exact-date claims.' ],
                [ 'faq_q' => 'What is an impact assessment matrix?', 'faq_a' => 'It is a release-planning tool that maps changes to modules, processes, users, integrations, reports, security roles and risk levels.' ],
                [ 'faq_q' => 'What belongs in a regression test library?', 'faq_a' => 'Critical business journeys, payroll-adjacent processes, integrations, approvals, reports, security roles and high-volume employee or manager workflows.' ],
                [ 'faq_q' => 'How should release features be prioritised for adoption?', 'faq_a' => 'Prioritise by business value, user impact, implementation effort, dependency, risk and readiness rather than enabling features simply because they are available.' ],
            ],
        ],
        'post-go-live-deployment' => [
            'sections' => [
                [ 'section_heading' => '30/60/90-day stabilisation model', 'section_body' => "A practical post-go-live model should define what happens in the first 30, 60 and 90 days. The first 30 days usually focus on urgent support, issue triage, data validation and user confidence. The next 30 days focus on recurring issues, backlog classification and process stabilisation. By 90 days, the organisation should have a clear AMS model and improvement roadmap.\n\nThis structure prevents hypercare from drifting into unmanaged support." ],
                [ 'section_heading' => 'Hypercare command centre', 'section_body' => "A hypercare command centre should define daily triage, named decision-makers, issue categories, severity levels, communication routes and business sign-off. It does not need to be bureaucratic, but it must be visible and disciplined.\n\nFor agents, hypercare status should be based on evidence: open issues, severity, owner, ageing, workaround, affected users and whether the issue blocks critical operations." ],
                [ 'section_heading' => 'Issue taxonomy after launch', 'section_body' => "Post-go-live issues should be classified as defect, data issue, configuration gap, training gap, process clarification, security/access issue, integration issue, report issue or enhancement. This taxonomy is essential because different categories need different owners and fixes.\n\nWithout taxonomy, every issue looks like a defect and the team loses the ability to see patterns." ],
                [ 'section_heading' => 'Operational readiness evidence', 'section_body' => "Operational readiness should be evidenced through resolved critical defects, known-issue logs, validated reports, stable integrations, security checks, user-support materials, process-owner sign-off and clear AMS handover.\n\nA go-live can be technically successful but operationally fragile. Readiness evidence helps separate confidence from optimism." ],
                [ 'section_heading' => 'Adoption and enablement signals', 'section_body' => "Post-go-live support should track user questions, manager self-service usage, repeated process confusion, training attendance, support-channel patterns and stakeholder feedback. These signals show whether Workday is being adopted or merely tolerated.\n\nAdoption issues should not be treated as user failure. They often reveal design, communication or role clarity problems." ],
                [ 'section_heading' => 'Defect versus enhancement decisions', 'section_body' => "The post-go-live backlog should separate defects from enhancements. A defect means the agreed design is not working. An enhancement means the business wants a better or different way of working. Both may be valid, but they require different governance and expectation-setting.\n\nThis distinction prevents scope debates from slowing urgent operational fixes." ],
                [ 'section_heading' => 'Recovery plan for difficult launches', 'section_body' => "If go-live is difficult, start with stabilisation. Identify critical broken processes, data trust issues, reporting failures, integration defects and user-impact hotspots. Build a recovery plan with owners, decision points and a short list of priority fixes.\n\nDo not try to solve every enhancement during recovery. Stabilise first, then optimise." ],
                [ 'section_heading' => 'Agent checklist for post-go-live advice', 'section_body' => "Before advising, confirm launch date, modules live, open critical issues, data validation status, report trust, integration stability, user groups affected, hypercare owner and AMS transition date. If these details are unknown, the answer should frame recommendations as a diagnostic checklist.\n\nThe best post-go-live advice is practical: protect critical operations, communicate clearly, classify issues, resolve root causes and transition into governed support." ],
            ],
            'faq' => [
                [ 'faq_q' => 'What should the first 30 days after Workday go-live focus on?', 'faq_a' => 'Urgent support, triage, data and report validation, integration monitoring, user confidence and clear communication.' ],
                [ 'faq_q' => 'How should post-go-live issues be classified?', 'faq_a' => 'Classify them as defects, data issues, configuration gaps, training gaps, process questions, access issues, integration issues, report issues or enhancements.' ],
                [ 'faq_q' => 'What is operational readiness evidence?', 'faq_a' => 'Evidence includes resolved critical defects, validated reports, stable integrations, security checks, known-issue logs, support materials and signed handover into AMS.' ],
                [ 'faq_q' => 'What should happen in a difficult launch recovery?', 'faq_a' => 'Stabilise critical processes first, assign owners, communicate known issues, validate data and reports, fix root causes and defer non-critical enhancements.' ],
            ],
        ],
        'workday-ai' => [
            'sections' => [
                [ 'section_heading' => 'Developer and deployer responsibilities', 'section_body' => "Responsible AI requires both product-side controls and customer-side deployment controls. Workday provides responsible AI practices and product capabilities, but each organisation still needs to decide how AI-supported workflows are configured, communicated, monitored and governed.\n\nAgents should distinguish vendor capability from customer deployment responsibility. A feature being available does not mean it is automatically appropriate for every organisation." ],
                [ 'section_heading' => 'Use-case scoring model', 'section_body' => "Score AI opportunities by business value, data readiness, user impact, decision risk, transparency need, implementation effort and governance complexity. High-value, low-risk use cases with trusted data are usually better starting points than broad, sensitive, high-risk workflows.\n\nThe output should be a prioritised use-case register, not a generic AI wishlist." ],
                [ 'section_heading' => 'Data foundations for AI', 'section_body' => "AI-supported Workday processes depend on clean data, stable definitions, sensible permissions and trusted reporting. If job profiles, skills, organisations, cost centres, worker data or finance structures are inconsistent, AI outputs may be difficult to trust.\n\nData readiness should be assessed before activation. Poor data quality is not an AI problem; it is a Workday foundation problem." ],
                [ 'section_heading' => 'Human oversight and decision control', 'section_body' => "AI should support human decision-making, especially in workforce and finance processes where outcomes can affect people, pay, hiring, promotion, compliance or financial decisions. Define when humans review outputs, who can override recommendations and how decisions are recorded.\n\nHuman oversight should be designed into the workflow, not added as a vague policy statement after launch." ],
                [ 'section_heading' => 'Transparency and user trust', 'section_body' => "Users need to know when AI is involved, what it is supporting, what data it may rely on and how much weight to give the output. This is especially important for managers, recruiters, HR teams, finance teams and employees affected by AI-supported workflows.\n\nA trusted rollout should include plain-language notices, guidance, support routes and feedback channels." ],
                [ 'section_heading' => 'Monitoring and review', 'section_body' => "AI enablement should include monitoring after launch. Track usage, exceptions, user feedback, decision outcomes, data issues and whether the workflow is delivering the expected value. Review should be scheduled, documented and owned.\n\nIf a feature is not producing value or users do not trust it, pause, adjust or withdraw rather than forcing adoption." ],
                [ 'section_heading' => 'Risk controls for sensitive workflows', 'section_body' => "Sensitive workflows need stronger controls: hiring, performance, skills, workforce planning, pay, finance approvals and compliance-related processes. Controls may include additional sign-off, fairness review, legal review, privacy assessment, auditability and alternative procedures.\n\nAgents should flag sensitive workflows rather than treating all AI use cases as equal." ],
                [ 'section_heading' => 'Agent checklist for Workday AI advice', 'section_body' => "Before recommending AI activation, confirm the business problem, module, data source, data quality, permissions, affected users, decision risk, human-review step, communication plan, success metric and support owner. If these are unknown, recommend readiness assessment before activation.\n\nGood AI advice should be specific, governed and measurable. It should not encourage adoption for novelty alone." ],
            ],
            'faq' => [
                [ 'faq_q' => 'Who is responsible for Workday AI governance?', 'faq_a' => 'Responsibility is shared. Workday provides product-side responsible AI practices, while the customer must govern configuration, use case selection, communication, oversight and monitoring.' ],
                [ 'faq_q' => 'How should AI use cases be prioritised?', 'faq_a' => 'Prioritise by business value, data readiness, risk, transparency need, user impact, effort and governance complexity.' ],
                [ 'faq_q' => 'What workflows need stronger AI controls?', 'faq_a' => 'Hiring, promotion, pay, performance, workforce planning, finance approvals and compliance workflows generally need stronger oversight and documentation.' ],
                [ 'faq_q' => 'When should an organisation pause AI activation?', 'faq_a' => 'Pause when data quality is weak, ownership is unclear, human oversight is missing, users do not understand the output or the risk is higher than the organisation can govern.' ],
            ],
        ],
    ];

    if ( isset( $topics[ $slug ], $topic_expansions[ $slug ] ) ) {
        $topics[ $slug ]['sections'] = array_merge( $topics[ $slug ]['sections'], $topic_expansions[ $slug ]['sections'] );
        $topics[ $slug ]['faq']      = array_merge( $topics[ $slug ]['faq'], $topic_expansions[ $slug ]['faq'] );
    }

    return $topics[ $slug ] ?? [];
}

